Array Transformation Using Subroutines in Assembly Language
1. Objectives
Aims to provide practical experience in using subroutines, loops,
and conditional statements in assembly language. You will work with an array of
integers and perform value transformations using structured looping and subroutine
calls.
2. Task
Write an assembly program that performs the following operations:
1. Initialize Array A:
Define an array A with five integer elements of your choice (e.g., {3,
8, 13, 18, 23} ).
Store these elements in consecutive memory addresses (e.g., starting
from $30 ).
2. Create a subroutine to calculate the difference:
Implement a subroutine that takes two parameters (two numbers from the
array) and returns their difference.
3. Process Array A:
Use a loop to iterate over each element of Array A.
If an element is less than 10, add 5 to that element using the
subroutine and store it in Array B. If it is greater than or equal to
10, subtract 7 and store the result in Array B.
4. Store results:
Store Array B in consecutive memory addresses (e.g., starting from
$40 ).
Additionally, store the final sum of all elements in Array B at a
specified memory location (e.g., $50 ).
3. Example Input and Output
Initial Array A (values in memory starting at $30 ): {3, 8, 13, 18, 23}
Expected Result in Array B (memory starting at $40 ): {8, 13, 6, 11, 16}
Sum of Array B Elements (stored at $50 ): 54
4. Hints
Use LDAA and STAA instructions to load and store individual array values.
Use SUBA for subtraction.
Use JSR to call the subroutine for calculating the difference between two
numbers.Utilize an index register (e.g., X or Y ) to iterate through Array A and store
results in Array B.
Implement branching and looping to process each array element.
Include comments to document the purpose of each major section of the code.
5. Program Requirements
Array A should be initialized in memory starting at address $30 .
Array B should store transformed values in memory starting at address $40 .
The sum of elements in B should be stored at memory address $50 .
Implement conditional checks to determine whether an element in A is less than
10.
Thorough commenting in your code improves readability and helps during grading.
